<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
	<parent>
		<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
		<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
		<version>4.1.0</version>
		<relativePath/> <!-- lookup parent from repository -->
	</parent>
	<groupId>com.chaconne-ai</groupId>
	<artifactId>openspreader</artifactId>
	<version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
	<name>openspreader</name>
	<description>
		A complete set of multi-process programming components for Spring Boot:
		distributed locks, semaphores, latches and barriers, a process pool, a
		replicated cache, cluster-wide scheduling, RPC and MapReduce-style aggregation,
		all on top of the embedded spreader cluster.

		Each component keeps the shape of its java.util.concurrent counterpart and
		spans every instance of the application instead of one JVM. A ProcessingMutex is
		acquired and released like a ReentrantLock; a ProcessingSemaphore hands out
		permits the same way; the process pool takes a method call on one instance and
		runs it on another. The cluster itself is spreader, running inside the same JVM
		as the application's beans, so there is no Redis, no ZooKeeper and no message
		broker in the picture.

		Every component has two scopes: one confined to instances of the same
		application, one spanning everything on the cluster port. The cache replicates by
		shipping operations rather than data, so a setbit on a 100MB bitmap costs one
		datagram and a cluster-wide Bloom filter becomes practical; every node holds a
		full replica, so reads never leave the process. Scheduled tasks fire on every
		instance but run on exactly one. The process pool over @MultiProcessingCall and
		remote calls over @RpcClient, which routes by application name rather than by
		URL, are plain annotations on plain beans.

		Every failure surfaces as ProcessingException, split by cause rather than summed
		into one rate. Metrics go to Prometheus and cluster health joins the actuator
		endpoints, carrying each member's HTTP address, so one node's health response is
		enough to reach any other.

		The limits of the underlying cluster are inherited rather than hidden:
		leadership is not consensus, and the cache is memory only. Under a network
		partition two sides can each hold a leader, and a full cluster restart starts
		from empty. If double execution is unacceptable, this is not the tool.

		Nothing is enabled by default. Every component is switched on through
		properties, so adding this starter to a running service changes nothing until
		one of them is asked for.
	</description>
